WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.170
- Do you struggle with taking notes

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.480
in an effective way at work?

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.520
In this video, I'm going to
share the note taking technique

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.670
that I developed over
the six years of working

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.650
at the consulting firm, McKinsey.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.560
I do believe that note
taking in an effective way

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.920
can really make the difference
for you and your career.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.310
So please watch until the end

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.620
for the tips that I have to
share here today with you

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.950
from all the experiences that I have

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.120
of really conducting literally
hundreds of interviews

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.790
with the clients and
internal and external experts

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:32.960
over the time of my career.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.540
So welcome everyone to
another coffee break

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.670
here on my channel Firm Learning

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:38.700
and as you may be see

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.840
I got myself some nice consulting merch.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.340
So please fix a nice Excel cup.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.890
If you are interested to
get this stuff as well

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:50.240
check out the merch of Crazy
Management Consultants.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.640
Maybe you've seen my
video about two weeks ago

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.680
on Crazy Management Consultant memes

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:55.672
they're so hilarious,

00:00:55.672 --> 00:00:58.020
so I thought that I'd just
get some merch as well.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.020
I will leave a link to their website below

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.000
in the video description so check it out.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.250
But guys, let's now
talk about note taking.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.560
And I wanna start by
sharing a little story

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.910
that really happened to me

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.200
when I was a young consultant at work,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.630
we had a project where we
needed to help a client

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.850
optimize its working capital.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.930
So pretty much optimize its inventory,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.200
accounts receivables
and accounts payables.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.920
Now we needed to conduct an interview

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.870
with the head of one of the
business units of the company

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.590
to sit together with them
and collect some ideas

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.990
and share some thoughts
on what could be done

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.270
in that the business unit

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.990
to really improve the
working capital situation.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.330
So I walked into the meeting

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.540
and probably this was in my
first year of being a consultant

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.090
and I was accompanied by
a more senior associate

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.230
with whom we together
conducted the interview.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.490
It was a good and long
conversation going on

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.080
for almost two hours.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.910
And I still remember my blog,

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:51.570
I had pages over pages of notes

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.010
that I had written down there

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.700
just trying to capture everything

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.900
pretty much of importance
that was being said.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.660
So we had this is good conversation.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.610
And I remember that we walked
back into the team room

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.270
and there of course our
project lead was sitting

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.790
and he was looking at us and asking,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.430
so, hey how did it went?

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:08.490
What did we learn?

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.830
So he looked at me and I
tried to give an answer

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.790
but I was so puzzled,

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.587
and I tried to look through my notes

00:02:14.587 --> 00:02:18.450
you know, to really come up
with the most important points

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:19.710
of what was discussed,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.780
because in my mind there
was just so much being said,

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.323
so many information that I put down

00:02:24.323 --> 00:02:26.650
that it was really
difficult for me now to,

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.400
on the spot really share the top ideas,

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.960
the top points that we shared.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.700
So seeing how much I struggled

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.100
the senior associate was jumping
in and try to help me out.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.500
And he just looked at his notes

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.420
and said something like,

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:39.987
so yes, we had a good conversation.

00:02:39.987 --> 00:02:42.100
And there were five great ideas

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.410
that we can now implement really

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:44.960
to improve the working capital.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.040
One, two, three, four, five,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.380
and then to each point
he was briefly describing

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.590
what the idea was.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.710
And after seeing this, how we handled that

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.570
I was just so astonished how he did this

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.820
because clearly he did
something completely different

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.570
in the that he took us notes

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.880
in the way that he
organized the information

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:03.973
while conducting the interview.

00:03:03.973 --> 00:03:06.090
That now enabled him to communicate

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.860
in this very effective way.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.277
So later I was just sitting with him

00:03:09.277 --> 00:03:10.550
and we had a good relationship,

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:11.390
so I just asked him,

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.410
hey can you just show
me what you did there?

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.680
How did you come up with that?

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.580
How are you taking your notes?

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.510
So then he showed me his system

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.240
and this is pretty much now the system

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.420
that I also took over and
refined a bit over time.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.730
And this is the system

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.710
that I will show you now in this video.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.070
But before I talk about
this approach in detail

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.180
let me give you the top three reasons

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.250
why taking notes in consulting

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.000
and probably also many other
professional jobs as well

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.160
is very important.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.280
Let me also share what
is specific about that.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.792
So first point is the
special circumstances

00:03:41.792 --> 00:03:44.100
that you are facing when
you take these notes.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.470
So I did some research
before I did this video

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.520
so I looked how other
how-to-take-notes videos

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.180
on YouTube look like

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.530
and many of them are about

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.410
how you can take these
beautifully looking,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.880
super well structured notes,

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.110
while I trust that this can be great

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.110
and effective as well as passionately

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.520
for studying or for taking
notes during university,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.280
this just does not work in consulting

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.190
and indeed probably

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:11.240
in most other professional
contexts as way.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.970
And the reason is that you are
under a lot of time pressure.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:15.650
So you're sitting in these meetings

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.010
and often your interview partner

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.760
the person that you talk
to will talk pretty fast

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.770
and you cannot just constantly
ask him to slow down

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.090
because you need to
write it more precisely,

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:26.850
you know, you want to call her your notes

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:28.040
or anything like that, right?

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.490
This just will not work.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.780
And also after the meeting,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.850
you will regularly not have the time

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.140
to go over all your notes in details

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.640
and try to rewrite them
or make them look good.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:38.760
Just check out my video

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.314
on how a typical work week

00:04:40.314 --> 00:04:42.410
of the day of a consultant looks like.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:43.940
Again I will link it somewhere above,

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.770
if you see that at first you'll understand

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.380
why consultants really not have time

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.040
to now detail rewrite all of them.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.460
So bottom line is you need a system

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:51.980
where you can take notes efficiently

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.400
you do not have the time
to make them look great.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.810
Second, there's a special
expectation in consulting

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.290
that especially as a junior

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.460
you pretty much take notes
all the time, always.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.990
Also, if it's about topics

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.080
that do not directly
relate to your work stream

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.910
to your specific module,
to your responsibilities.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:11.800
So very often you will be in a situation

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.710
where maybe a client
walks into the team room

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.280
and is having a conversation
with the project lead.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.060
And then the client talks
about different things

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.160
and of course the project lead

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.330
tries to engage with
them as much as possible.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.070
And then very often you will
find yourself in a situation

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.210
where when the client left

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.210
the project lead will look to you and say,

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.830
Hey, Heinrich you took notes
of everything he said, right?

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.050
And then you are like sitting there.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:32.890
Maybe you even feel like

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.307
that wasn't even about my work stream,

00:05:34.307 --> 00:05:36.470
why should I have taken notes about that?

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:37.693
So for instance, also a team call

00:05:37.693 --> 00:05:40.700
where a partner talks about
something, doing something

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.747
maybe you believe that probably
it's not going to be you

00:05:42.747 --> 00:05:44.400
who will actually implement it.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.050
But what if maybe at the end of the call

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:47.580
or maybe later in a team meeting,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.590
the project lead asks you if you can do

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.170
that specific piece of work

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.040
that now the partner talked about earlier

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.470
and now you will look pretty bad

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:55.646
if you pretty much just have to say,

00:05:55.646 --> 00:05:58.090
okay but I didn't take notes.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.820
Can we please go over it again?

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.150
What exactly I should do?

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:04.130
So again, situations like
this come up all the time.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.610
So pretty much a good advice

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:06.700
for young consultants is

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:09.840
whenever information of
significance are being shared

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.200
by partners, by your
project lead or by clients

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.040
doesn't matter whether
they're directly related

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.310
to your work stream or not

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:16.957
always take notes.

00:06:16.957 --> 00:06:18.540
And the third thing that is special

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.130
about taking notes in consulting

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.560
is that often, especially
in client meetings

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.820
you will try to really take
the notes with pen and paper

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:27.050
but not just with a laptop.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:30.610
So I know there are these
great note taking programs

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.330
like OneNote or Evernote,

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:34.880
I use them myself or other
applications as well.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.140
But especially in consulting,

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.810
when you sit in front of a client,

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.350
if you have a laptop in front of you

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:41.900
and are typing down
every word that he says

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.470
often this will create
a big perceived barrier

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:46.079
between you and the client.

00:06:46.079 --> 00:06:48.106
It will also often make
the whole situation

00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:50.080
much more formal than it is.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.840
Maybe the client we closed down,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:52.980
shut down a little bit

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.040
and will not talk that openly with you

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.130
because it feels like
every word that he says

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.970
is now taken down on a protocol.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.090
So for these reasons,

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:01.290
it is a common point

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:03.310
in these coinciding onboarding trainings

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:05.170
and also the general way of working

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:08.240
that you will try to take
notes, pen and paper.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.480
This will often help you to
communicate with your client

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:11.840
in a much better way.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:12.673
And for this reason, of course,

00:07:12.673 --> 00:07:14.790
you need to have a system
that also enables you

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.870
to take the notes in
a pen and paper format

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.240
and not only on a computer.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.930
And now let's get tangible.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:20.980
How do I do it?

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:22.760
What is this note taking system

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.640
that I really refined and also
implemented in my daily job?

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:27.900
And I prepared here a sheet of paper

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.290
that really shows this.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:31.620
So I hope now that auto focus switches.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.290
So you see that there
are different sections

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:35.830
on this piece of paper.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.290
So I try to, not only just make
a long list of bullet points

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:41.870
but really organize the information.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.230
Now because I know that's
probably pretty hard to follow

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.200
just based on this,

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.210
let me now sketch it for you,

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.910
how exactly it looks like.

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.670
I will use my PC for that.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.090
So let's now go over our format

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:53.880
how exactly this could look like.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:56.000
So this is now a big piece of paper.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.170
And as you see now, how you often start

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.310
by putting the date when
the meeting took place

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:02.370
in the top left,

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.460
then I often also like to put the title,

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:06.060
so what was it about?

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:07.970
And then I also like to include a list

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:09.280
of all the participants.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.521
This is also important whenever

00:08:10.521 --> 00:08:12.240
when you write meeting minutes

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.400
or any type of protocol after the meeting

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.000
that it's often good practice
to also write it down

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.440
who was part of the meeting.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.580
So make sure that your
list everybody down.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.920
Then a little thing that I
really found useful for myself,

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.120
it's not necessarily something

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:26.540
that I've seen other people do.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.910
But what I really found useful

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.560
is to insert a little drawing of the table

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.750
and then write the initials
of who was sitting where,

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.270
and for me, this really helped

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:37.610
especially in the beginning of a project

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:39.300
where you have lots of different meetings

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.600
and can easily get to know lots of people

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.240
in a very short amount of time.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:44.730
For me, it was always difficult

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:47.110
after a couple of days
to really remembering.

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.620
So who was who just based on the names

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.150
but now with these little sketches,

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:52.410
these really helped me to get

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.920
a bit of a visual
representation of the table

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:56.000
then back into my mind,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.090
the knowing okay, Mr. So-and-so

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.720
was the guy who was sitting
at the end of the table

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:01.830
in this specific meeting.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:04.290
So this is just a little
mind tag that I use

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.600
to just help me really
remembering who was who.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.580
So then you see the
upper half of the paper,

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.820
this is now kind of the general area.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.420
The general note taking
area where just write

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.200
kind of general remarks
of what was being said.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.390
So this is the part that
now most closely resembles

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.050
this general old school style

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:24.440
of just taking notes, one
point after the other.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.340
And of course, if you do run out of space

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.410
you're gonna, would just
take a second piece of paper

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:31.170
at some point is actually
just to continue writing down

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.290
these bullets as you see here.

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:35.130
Now, the more interesting
part is here, the lower part,

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:37.790
and on the very bottom quarter of the page

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:39.340
I often use two sections.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:41.760
So to-dos for me, to-dos
this for the firm,

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.010
so for our consulting firm,

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:44.760
and then to-dos for the client.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.090
So in the conversation,

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:47.930
whenever something was coming up

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:50.440
where either myself or
later as a project lead,

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:52.440
me and or my team needs to do something,

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:54.630
I will directly write it down here,

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:55.890
not in the upper part

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.620
but then also whenever there was something

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:00.370
where the client confirmed
and committed to do something

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:01.557
I would also write it down here.

00:10:01.557 --> 00:10:04.785
And this was always great
because in the end of the meeting

00:10:04.785 --> 00:10:07.230
then you can do something
really powerful and say,

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:10.020
okay now let me just recap,
what are the next steps,

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.390
and then you can talk
about what you will do now

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:13.810
but then you can also talk about

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:15.370
what the client will do now.

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:16.890
Because otherwise in a long meeting

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:19.370
you often have the situation
that in-between you say,

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:21.120
okay this is something that you can do

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:22.318
and then maybe the client agrees,

00:10:22.318 --> 00:10:25.470
but in the very end probably
both of you have forgotten.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:27.566
And if we will have them
pages of notes written out,

00:10:27.566 --> 00:10:30.670
it can be quite tough to
really find these to-do points.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:32.720
It's really helpful to
have them in one place

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:34.380
because then you can recap in the end

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:35.840
and you can again ask the client

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:37.110
to commit to these to-dos.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:38.950
And then of course also maybe afterwards

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:40.490
you wanna write short meeting minutes

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:42.107
and then you already
have this list of, to-dos

00:10:42.107 --> 00:10:43.830
and you do not need to search them

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:45.670
among pages of pages of notes.

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:47.810
They are just hidden in the
middle of lots of other texts.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.210
Now, next here in the
middle part of the page

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:51.043
you see an area where

00:10:51.043 --> 00:10:53.310
you can really take lots of value

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:55.600
in the situations that I was describing

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:56.640
in the story earlier.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.830
And this requires a
little bit of preparation

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:00.880
before you walk into the meeting

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.170
and this preparation is asking yourself

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.690
what you actually wanna
get out of the interview.

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:07.450
And this is indeed also a mistake

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:09.480
that some more junior consultants do

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.410
they just walk into these client meetings,

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:12.300
they walk into these interviews.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.800
And they're just having
a general discussion

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.510
about a topic with a client

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.200
but without a clear goal in mind,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:20.190
what is your objective?

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.580
What do you wanna take
out of the conversation?

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:24.050
Now, coming back to the example

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.230
of the working capital reduction project.

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:27.460
So what do you want to be looking for

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.260
from these meeting as a
list of tangible ideas

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:32.310
that now you hope that the
client will share with you

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.110
of what could be done to improve

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:35.790
the working capital of a company.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:37.190
And so indeed if you know

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.580
before the meeting that this
is what you're looking out for

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:40.513
try to make a section

00:11:40.513 --> 00:11:43.130
that whenever the client says something

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:44.700
that could be used as an idea,

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.210
that could be used as a
lever in this direction

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:49.040
where you directly write it down.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.050
Because this now is really powerful

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:52.453
whenever afterwards, somebody asks you,

00:11:52.453 --> 00:11:55.090
well what are you actually
taking out of this conversation?

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:58.770
You can list exactly these
reasons, these levers

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.980
these ideas that you
collected in the meeting.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:02.120
So now that being said,

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:04.210
what do you use, the bigger part of front,

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:05.990
where here you write on all the things

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:08.520
that are neither to do
for you or the client,

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:10.700
and are also not directly applicable

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:12.860
to this main objective of
the meeting that you have

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:14.840
for instance, collecting
optimization ideas.

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:16.910
So this could be just other general points

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:19.313
of information that you
find worthwhile taking notes

00:12:19.313 --> 00:12:20.720
that the client shares,

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:21.710
pretty much everything else

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.640
that somehow seems to be relevant

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:24.700
but you do not wanna add

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.870
into one of these other
buckets that we have below.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.320
So trust me, if you
employ a system like this

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.000
for your specific situation as well,

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.453
it will be far more organized

00:12:32.453 --> 00:12:35.830
than 99% of most people walking outside

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:37.200
and doing these kinds of interviews.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.860
I hope this was indeed
helpful and valuable.

